{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfqd8sGds3vDcdBpWRDuw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The verdict is in and committed as `25a72ad5` on `wi-864`, with the `WI: WI-864` trailer. The outcome is **QUEUE-WITH-EDGE** (`needs=WI-848 absorbs=-`). My first commit put the `WI:` line in its own paragraph, so git didn't read it as a trailer. I amended that unpushed commit, and git now reads it.\n\n**Your question: do these rows guard the same thing from several directions?** No. They form one pipeline cut into stages, and each stage is a separate decision:\n\n| Stage | Rows |\n|---|---|\n| What a reviewer reports | WI-852 (asks for failure classes), WI-860 (the threat model limits scope) |\n| Every finding must be covered by the rework plan | WI-853 |\n| Contested or repeated findings go to the adjudicator | WI-811 |\n| A builder that keeps failing gets a new plan, then a higher tier | WI-805 |\n| Cheaper review rounds | WI-847 |\n| The coordinator's procedure, written down | WI-848 |\n\nWI-854 is outside the theme: it judges spine rows, not review findings. I found no contradiction with the spine and nothing already answered, so nothing should be merged.\n\nThe real risk is that several facts get two homes, and ordering fixes that:\n- **The threat model:** the coordinator's brief templates restate it today, and WI-860 makes PROCESS.md §6 its one home.\n- **The scope-critique questions:** WI-848's draft skill lists them, and WI-852 moves them into a rubric.\n- **The sweep table:** WI-848's draft describes it, and WI-853 makes it a checked format.\n\n**Edges the close will add:** WI-848 waits on WI-852, WI-853 and WI-860. Otherwise the coordinator-cycle skill could land describing templates and gates those rows are about to change or retire. Nothing waits on WI-848, so only WI-848 is delayed. I checked that the close machinery accepts the verdict and that WI-848's `needs` line comes out as `[\"WI-846\", \"WI-852\", \"WI-853\", \"WI-860\"]`.\n\n**Two edges you need to add by hand.** The close can't add them because neither row has a `needs` line (that's the gap WI-856 fixes):\n- **WI-852 needs WI-860.** WI-852 retires the coordinator's brief templates and moves what they carry into the kit templates. They now carry the threat model word for word, so WI-852 would plant a second copy in the shipped reviewer template, and WI-860 would then have to remove it.\n- **WI-853 needs WI-852.** The coordinator's reviews are in a hand format until WI-852 lands. If WI-853 goes first, it has to parse that format too, which is a legacy path that gets retired straight afterwards. This edge doesn't delay WI-805, which already waits on WI-852.\n\nAdd both before claiming any of those rows, or land WI-856 first.\n\n**Smaller items, written up in the verdict:**\n- **WI-848:** add a Done-when bullet so the skill carries your 2026-10-08 threat-model ruling. The verdict has the bullet text; the draft was written before the ruling.\n- **WI-811:** two rules act at the third rejected review round. WI-805 says build again at a higher tier; WI-811 says take a contested finding to the adjudicator first. WI-811's builder should state that the adjudicator sitting comes first in the automated loop too, and that the higher-tier build answers only the findings the adjudicator kept.\n- **WI-811** should also update the coordinator-cycle skill's dispute step when it lands.\n\nThe `.requested` file in the review folder is still uncommitted; I left it for the adjudication entry point to commit. I didn't move my own spec or touch any of the eight rows."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":913,"cache_read_input_tokens":137857,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":913},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],